<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Industrial Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1760–<a href="/years/1840">1840</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Industrial Revolution was the transition to mechanised production, steam power and factory industry that began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the eighteenth century. It greatly increased Britain's productive and military capacity. It provided the economic foundation for the expansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> and shaped its trade with colonies as sources of raw materials and markets.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution.jpg" alt="Industrial Revolution" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Illustrator T. Allom, Engraver J. Tingle · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Powerloom_weaving_in_1835.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Industrial Revolution</b>, which began in Britain around the 1760s and accelerated through the early nineteenth century, gave the empire an economic lead that no competitor could close within a generation: by about 1850 Britain was producing roughly half of the world's iron and cotton cloth, while its…</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 1760s the <b>Industrial Revolution</b> began, with James Hargreaves’s spinning jenny, Richard Arkwright’s water frame and factories, James Watt’s improved steam engine, patented in 1769, and canals such as the Bridgewater Canal of 1761.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The great factories of the <b>Industrial Revolution</b> and the immense growth in coal burning brought air pollution on a scale not seen before to industrial centres, and after 1900 industrial chemical discharges added to the load of untreated human waste.</blockquote><a href="/movement/environmental-movement">Environmental movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Industrial Revolution</b>, which began in Britain in the late 18th century, transformed work.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Modernism developed out of Romanticism’s revolt against the effects of the <b>Industrial Revolution</b> and against bourgeois values; for the literary scholar Gerald Graff its ground motive was criticism of the 19th-century bourgeois social order.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Industrial Revolution</b>, with its factories and swelling cities, made untouched nature and the rural past seem precious; William Blake wrote of England’s “dark Satanic Mills”.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Background</a></li></ul></section>
